1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,685
Three photos stuck in your Downloads folder.

2
00:00:02,685 --> 00:00:03,017
Watch.

3
00:00:03,017 --> 00:00:05,785
One line, and they're all moved into a new folder.

4
00:00:05,785 --> 00:00:07,169
No dragging, no clicking.

5
00:00:07,169 --> 00:00:13,314
That's the command line, and in the next few minutes you'll learn the commands everyone should know, from zero.

6
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:20,176
The command line looks scary, but it's just a window where you type instructions instead of clicking.

7
00:00:20,176 --> 00:00:24,081
About a dozen commands cover almost everything a beginner needs.

8
00:00:24,081 --> 00:00:26,034
Let's learn them, one at a time.

9
00:00:26,734 --> 00:00:28,742
Why bother, when you have a mouse?

10
00:00:28,742 --> 00:00:31,401
Because typing is faster for repetitive jobs.

11
00:00:31,401 --> 00:00:33,587
One command can move a hundred files.

12
00:00:33,587 --> 00:00:39,022
It's also how you run Python scripts, use Git, and follow almost every programming tutorial.

13
00:00:39,022 --> 00:00:42,507
Once you know the basics, none of that feels scary anymore.

14
00:00:43,207 --> 00:00:44,162
Here's the plan.

15
00:00:44,162 --> 00:00:45,116
Open a terminal.

16
00:00:45,116 --> 00:00:47,501
Find out where you are and what's there.

17
00:00:47,501 --> 00:00:48,753
Move between folders.

18
00:00:48,753 --> 00:00:50,900
Create, copy, move and delete files.

19
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:55,312
And then the errors you'll see, and two shortcuts that make it all faster.

20
00:00:56,012 --> 00:00:57,233
First, open a terminal.

21
00:00:57,233 --> 00:01:00,525
On a Mac, press command space, type terminal, and press enter.

22
00:01:00,525 --> 00:01:02,862
On Linux, it's called Terminal in your apps.

23
00:01:02,862 --> 00:01:07,906
On Windows, the easiest way to follow along is Git Bash, which comes free with Git for Windows.

24
00:01:07,906 --> 00:01:09,764
We installed it in the Git episode.

25
00:01:09,764 --> 00:01:15,605
Windows PowerShell understands some of these commands too, but not all of them, and the options are different.

26
00:01:16,305 --> 00:01:17,063
A quick note.

27
00:01:17,063 --> 00:01:20,326
The dollar sign at the start of each line is the prompt.

28
00:01:20,326 --> 00:01:22,716
It means the terminal is waiting for you.

29
00:01:22,716 --> 00:01:23,765
You don't type it.

30
00:01:23,765 --> 00:01:27,845
On a Mac the prompt ends with a percent sign instead, and that's fine.

31
00:01:27,845 --> 00:01:30,351
Every command in this video works the same.

32
00:01:31,051 --> 00:01:33,623
The terminal is always inside one folder.

33
00:01:33,623 --> 00:01:35,442
To see which one, type p w d.

34
00:01:35,442 --> 00:01:40,773
It stands for print working directory, and directory is just another word for folder.

35
00:01:40,773 --> 00:01:42,780
Mine says slash home slash alex.

36
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:46,355
That's my home folder, where the terminal usually starts.

37
00:01:47,055 --> 00:01:48,772
Now, what's in this folder?

38
00:01:48,772 --> 00:01:50,362
Type l s, short for list.

39
00:01:50,362 --> 00:01:55,004
You see Desktop, Documents, Downloads, and a file called notes dot t x t.

40
00:01:55,004 --> 00:01:58,947
The same things you'd see in your file explorer, just as text.

41
00:01:59,647 --> 00:02:05,029
Add a space and dash l, for long, and you get one item per line, with more details.

42
00:02:05,029 --> 00:02:08,401
Lines starting with a d are directories, so folders.

43
00:02:08,401 --> 00:02:12,811
Then the owner, the size in bytes, and the date it was last changed.

44
00:02:12,811 --> 00:02:15,015
Notes dot t x t is eighteen bytes.

45
00:02:15,715 --> 00:02:18,978
And l s dash a shows all files, including hidden ones.

46
00:02:18,978 --> 00:02:23,811
Hidden files start with a dot, like dot bash r c, which holds terminal settings.

47
00:02:23,811 --> 00:02:29,731
You'll also see a single dot, which means this folder, and two dots, which means the folder above.

48
00:02:29,731 --> 00:02:31,181
Remember those two dots.

49
00:02:31,181 --> 00:02:32,812
We'll use them in a second.

50
00:02:33,512 --> 00:02:34,606
Notice the pattern.

51
00:02:34,606 --> 00:02:36,506
Every command has the same shape.

52
00:02:36,506 --> 00:02:38,233
First the command, the action.

53
00:02:38,233 --> 00:02:41,803
Then options, which start with a dash and change how it works.

54
00:02:41,803 --> 00:02:45,028
Then the argument, which is what you want it to work on.

55
00:02:45,028 --> 00:02:46,525
Command, option, argument.

56
00:02:46,525 --> 00:02:49,115
That's the grammar of the whole command line.

57
00:02:49,815 --> 00:02:54,311
To go into a folder, type c d, for change directory, then the folder name.

58
00:02:54,311 --> 00:02:55,162
c d Documents.

59
00:02:55,162 --> 00:02:57,470
Nothing is printed, and that's normal.

60
00:02:57,470 --> 00:03:01,601
Type p w d, and you're now in slash home slash alex slash Documents.

61
00:03:01,601 --> 00:03:03,363
And l s shows what's in here.

62
00:03:03,363 --> 00:03:05,611
A resume, and a folder called school.

63
00:03:06,311 --> 00:03:08,528
Here's what that looks like as a map.

64
00:03:08,528 --> 00:03:10,505
c d school goes one level deeper.

65
00:03:10,505 --> 00:03:13,980
c d, space, dot dot, goes up one level, back to Documents.

66
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:17,036
And c d, tilde, jumps straight home, from anywhere.

67
00:03:17,036 --> 00:03:21,530
The tilde is the little wave key, usually at the top left of your keyboard.

68
00:03:22,230 --> 00:03:24,825
And here it is in the real terminal.

69
00:03:24,825 --> 00:03:27,996
After each c d, p w d confirms where we are.

70
00:03:27,996 --> 00:03:30,879
School, then Documents again, then home.

71
00:03:31,579 --> 00:03:34,561
You can also jump several levels at once.

72
00:03:34,561 --> 00:03:38,998
c d Documents slash school goes two folders deep in one step.

73
00:03:38,998 --> 00:03:42,562
And c d dot dot slash dot dot goes up two levels.

74
00:03:42,562 --> 00:03:43,726
Back home again.

75
00:03:44,426 --> 00:03:46,043
One idea makes paths click.

76
00:03:46,043 --> 00:03:51,133
A path that starts with a slash is a full address, from the very top of the computer.

77
00:03:51,133 --> 00:03:53,649
Slash home slash alex works from anywhere.

78
00:03:53,649 --> 00:03:59,517
A path without a slash at the start is relative, so it starts from the folder you're in right now.

79
00:04:00,217 --> 00:04:01,682
Let's build something.

80
00:04:01,682 --> 00:04:03,146
We're planning a trip.

81
00:04:03,146 --> 00:04:05,275
m k dir trip makes a new folder.

82
00:04:05,275 --> 00:04:06,739
That's make directory.

83
00:04:06,739 --> 00:04:08,137
c d trip goes inside.

84
00:04:08,137 --> 00:04:12,596
Then touch, followed by two names, creates two empty files at once.

85
00:04:12,596 --> 00:04:15,258
Packing dot t x t, and budget dot t x t.

86
00:04:15,258 --> 00:04:16,589
l s shows them both.

87
00:04:17,289 --> 00:04:19,222
To put text in a file, use echo.

88
00:04:19,222 --> 00:04:23,087
echo passport, then a greater than sign, then packing dot t x t.

89
00:04:23,087 --> 00:04:26,168
The greater than sign sends the text into the file.

90
00:04:26,168 --> 00:04:28,946
Then two greater than signs to add more lines.

91
00:04:28,946 --> 00:04:29,792
Phone charger.

92
00:04:29,792 --> 00:04:30,396
Sunscreen.

93
00:04:30,396 --> 00:04:32,510
And cat shows what's inside a file.

94
00:04:32,510 --> 00:04:34,322
Our packing list, three lines.

95
00:04:35,021 --> 00:04:36,312
Careful with this one.

96
00:04:36,312 --> 00:04:39,773
A single greater than sign replaces everything in the file.

97
00:04:39,773 --> 00:04:41,356
Two of them add to the end.

98
00:04:41,356 --> 00:04:43,409
Mix them up, and your list is gone.

99
00:04:43,409 --> 00:04:44,758
When in doubt, use two.

100
00:04:45,458 --> 00:04:50,506
To copy a file, type c p, then the file, then the name of the copy.

101
00:04:50,506 --> 00:04:54,500
c p packing dot t x t, packing dash backup dot t x t.

102
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:55,856
Now there are two.

103
00:04:56,556 --> 00:04:59,318
To rename, use m v, which means move.

104
00:04:59,318 --> 00:05:02,155
m v budget dot t x t, costs dot t x t.

105
00:05:02,155 --> 00:05:08,277
In the terminal, renaming is just moving a file to a new name, in the same folder.

106
00:05:08,977 --> 00:05:11,614
And m v also moves files between folders.

107
00:05:11,614 --> 00:05:13,672
That's the trick from the start.

108
00:05:13,672 --> 00:05:15,152
I make a photos folder.

109
00:05:15,152 --> 00:05:19,268
l s tilde slash Downloads shows three beach photos and a ticket.

110
00:05:19,268 --> 00:05:23,578
Then m v, tilde slash Downloads slash star dot j p g, photos slash.

111
00:05:23,578 --> 00:05:27,823
All three photos move in one go, and only the ticket stays behind.

112
00:05:28,523 --> 00:05:30,368
The star is a wildcard.

113
00:05:30,368 --> 00:05:31,813
It means anything.

114
00:05:31,813 --> 00:05:36,467
So star dot j p g means every file that ends in dot j p g.

115
00:05:36,467 --> 00:05:38,553
It works with any command.

116
00:05:38,553 --> 00:05:42,163
l s star dot t x t lists only the text files.

117
00:05:42,863 --> 00:05:45,629
To delete a file, type r m, for remove.

118
00:05:45,629 --> 00:05:48,040
r m packing dash backup dot t x t.

119
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:48,395
Gone.

120
00:05:48,395 --> 00:05:50,593
And when I say gone, I mean it.

121
00:05:50,593 --> 00:05:52,365
More on that in a second.

122
00:05:53,065 --> 00:05:54,798
r m won't delete a folder.

123
00:05:54,798 --> 00:05:57,264
Cannot remove photos, is a directory.

124
00:05:57,264 --> 00:06:01,930
The command for folders is r m dir, but it only removes empty folders.

125
00:06:01,930 --> 00:06:05,262
Our photos folder still has photos, so it refuses.

126
00:06:05,262 --> 00:06:06,595
Directory not empty.

127
00:06:06,595 --> 00:06:10,461
An empty folder, like this one called old, goes away fine.

128
00:06:11,161 --> 00:06:14,132
Here's the most important warning in this video.

129
00:06:14,132 --> 00:06:15,804
r m does not use the trash.

130
00:06:15,804 --> 00:06:16,794
There's no undo.

131
00:06:16,794 --> 00:06:20,322
And r m dash r deletes a folder and everything inside it.

132
00:06:20,322 --> 00:06:26,017
So before you delete, run l s with the same name first, and check what's going to disappear.

133
00:06:26,717 --> 00:06:28,593
Now the errors you'll see most.

134
00:06:28,593 --> 00:06:32,464
No such file or directory, usually means a typo, like Documnets.

135
00:06:32,464 --> 00:06:35,307
Or you're in a different folder than you think.

136
00:06:35,307 --> 00:06:39,723
Command not found means the command name is misspelled, or not installed.

137
00:06:39,723 --> 00:06:43,473
And file exists means there's already a folder with that name.

138
00:06:44,173 --> 00:06:46,020
So, three errors, three fixes.

139
00:06:46,020 --> 00:06:49,777
Check the spelling, and remember that capital letters matter.

140
00:06:49,777 --> 00:06:53,225
Documents with a capital D is not the same as documents.

141
00:06:53,225 --> 00:06:56,058
Run p w d and l s to see where you really are.

142
00:06:56,058 --> 00:06:59,691
And pick a new name, or use the folder that already exists.

143
00:07:00,391 --> 00:07:01,257
One more trap.

144
00:07:01,257 --> 00:07:02,246
Spaces in names.

145
00:07:02,246 --> 00:07:09,482
c d summer plans fails with too many arguments, because the terminal thinks summer and plans are two separate things.

146
00:07:09,482 --> 00:07:11,770
Put the name in quotes, and it works.

147
00:07:12,470 --> 00:07:13,621
Pro tip number one.

148
00:07:13,621 --> 00:07:15,378
Never type a full name again.

149
00:07:15,378 --> 00:07:18,770
Type the first few letters, like c d Doc, and press Tab.

150
00:07:18,770 --> 00:07:22,708
The terminal finishes the name for you, and it never makes typos.

151
00:07:22,708 --> 00:07:26,281
If nothing happens, press Tab twice to see all the options.

152
00:07:26,981 --> 00:07:28,111
Pro tip number two.

153
00:07:28,111 --> 00:07:33,402
Press the up arrow to bring back your last command, and keep pressing to go further back.

154
00:07:33,402 --> 00:07:36,256
Type history to see the full list, with numbers.

155
00:07:36,256 --> 00:07:41,369
Here, the vertical bar sends the list into tail, which keeps only the last five lines.

156
00:07:42,069 --> 00:07:45,297
And when you forget what a command can do, ask it.

157
00:07:45,297 --> 00:07:48,073
l s dash dash help prints the instructions.

158
00:07:48,073 --> 00:07:51,946
On a Mac, type man, space, l s instead, and press q to quit.

159
00:07:51,946 --> 00:07:55,368
And if a command ever gets stuck, control C stops it.

160
00:07:56,068 --> 00:07:56,871
Quick recap.

161
00:07:56,871 --> 00:07:58,075
p w d, where am I.

162
00:07:58,075 --> 00:07:59,212
l s, what's here.

163
00:07:59,212 --> 00:08:00,416
c d, go somewhere.

164
00:08:00,416 --> 00:08:02,155
m k dir and touch, create.

165
00:08:02,155 --> 00:08:04,094
echo and cat, write and read.

166
00:08:04,094 --> 00:08:04,763
c p, copy.

167
00:08:04,763 --> 00:08:06,101
m v, move or rename.

168
00:08:06,101 --> 00:08:07,906
And r m, delete, carefully.

169
00:08:07,906 --> 00:08:11,117
Pause the video and screenshot this cheat sheet.

170
00:08:11,817 --> 00:08:14,691
